  "answer": "I'll start by inspecting the workspace.\nRepair complete.\n\n## Changes (`src/calc.ts`)\n```ts\nexport function average(values: number[]): number {\n  const total: number = values.reduce((sum, value) => sum + value, 0)\n  if (values.length === 0) return 0\n  return total / values.length\n}\n```\n\nTwo edits, both in the body only (exported signature `average(values: number[]): number` unchanged):\n\n1. `const total: string` → `const total: number` — `Array.prototype.reduce` seeded with `0` over `number[]` produces a number, so the annotation was the actual type inconsistency.\n2. `Number(total) / values.length` → `total / values.length` — the `Number()` coercion was only masking the wrong annotation and is now unnecessary.\n\nThe empty-array guard intentionally stays before the division so `average([])` returns `0` rather than `NaN`.\n\n## Verification\n- `bun test` → 1 pass, 0 fail, 2 `expect()` calls, exit 0.\n- `tsc --noEmit --strict` on `src/calc.ts` → clean, exit 0 (invoked with explicit flags only; no config file or runner changes made).\n\n`AGENTS.md`, `package.json`, and `test/oracle.test.ts` were left untouched."
